answer.raw_answer:
{
  "answer": "When a Kotlin function called from Swift/Objective-C throws an exception, it depends on whether the function has the @Throws annotation and its nature (suspend or non-suspend):\n\n1. **Functions with @Throws annotation**: If the function or its ancestor throws an exception that is an instance of the specified @Throws class or a subclass, the exception is propagated as an NSError. For suspend functions with @Throws, this exception appears as an NSError in the completion handler[1].\n\n2. **Non-suspend functions without @Throws**: These functions do not propagate Kotlin exceptions at all[1].\n\n3. **Suspend functions without @Throws**: These propagate only CancellationException as an NSError[1].\n\nNote that this is one-way translation; the opposite (importing Swift/Objective-C error-throwing methods to Kotlin as exception-throwing) is not implemented yet[1].\n\nVersion caveat: The above information comes from the provided snapshot, and current guide snapshots do not guarantee historical version compatibility; release versions are not feature availability[1].",
  "clarification": "",
  "quotes": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "6395dce2f8c3c5d8b8dd9e9585788c35597612b6f05b537e6c446784e5cb1bbc",
      "text": "When a Kotlin function called from Swift/Objective-C code throws an exception which is an instance of one of the classes specified with @Throws or their subclasses, the exception is propagated as an NSError . Other Kotlin exceptions reaching Swift/Objective-C are considered unhandled and cause program termination."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "6395dce2f8c3c5d8b8dd9e9585788c35597612b6f05b537e6c446784e5cb1bbc",
      "text": "suspend functions without @Throws propagate only CancellationException (as NSError ). Non- suspend functions without @Throws don't propagate Kotlin exceptions at all."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "6395dce2f8c3c5d8b8dd9e9585788c35597612b6f05b537e6c446784e5cb1bbc",
      "text": "Note that the opposite reversed translation is not implemented yet: Swift/Objective-C error-throwing methods aren't imported to Kotlin as exception-throwing."
    }
  ],
  "status": "answered"
}

